# draco-noise-cancellation.skill - Phase 6: NLP-Powered Noise Cancellation & Terminal Stripping

## Purpose
Phase 6 skill for NLP-powered noise cancellation and terminal stripping. Achieves 70%+ noise token reduction without losing technical content.

## spaCy NER-Based Terminal Output Classification (Phase 6 Component 1)

### Named Entity Recognition Configuration
- `spacy_model": "en_core_web_sm" (spaCy model size: "en_core_web_sm", "en_core_web_md", "en_core/web_lg", default: "en_core_web_sm")
- `ner_entity_types": 20 (number of entity types to classify, range: 5-50, default: 20)
- `ner_confidence_threshold": 0.5 (minimum confidence for NER classification, range: 0.1-0.9, default: 0.5)
- `custom_entities": [] (custom entity patterns to add to spaCy NER, default: [])

### 20+ Entity Types for Terminal Output Classification
| Entity Type | Description | Example | Retention Priority |
|-------------|-------------|---------|-------------------|
| ANSI_CODE | ANSI escape codes | `\x1b[31m`, `\x1b[0m` | Strip (0) |
| PROGRESS_BAR | Progress indicator spinners | `⠋`, `⠙`, `⠹` | Strip (0) |
| PERCENTAGE | Percentage values | `50%`, `75.5%` | Keep (8) |
| ERROR_MESSAGE | Error output lines | `Error:`, `FailedError` | Keep (9) |
| WARNING_MESSAGE | Warning output lines | `Warning:`, `DeprecationWarning` | Keep (8) |
| STATUS_LINE | Status bar updates | `Processing...`, `Step 1/10` | Strip (1) |
| TIMESTAMP | Date/time stamps | `2024-01-15 10:30:00` | Strip (2) |
| PATH_FILE | File paths | `/home/user/file.py`, `C:\docs\report.pdf` | Keep (7) |
| COMMAND_OUTPUT | Command stdout/stderr | `Hello World`, `Error: file not found` | Keep (9) |
| NUMERIC_VALUE | Numeric values | `123`, `3.14159`, `0` | Keep (6) |
| IP_ADDRESS | IP addresses | `192.168.1.1`, `10.0.0.1` | Keep (7) |
| USERNAME | Usernames | `john_doe`, `admin` | Keep (5) |
| HOSTNAME | Hostnames | `localhost`, `server01` | Keep (6) |
| DATE | Date values | `Jan 15, 2024`, `2024-01-15` | Strip (3) |
| TIME | Time values | `10:30 AM`, `14:55` | Strip (3) |
| DURATION | Time durations | `2h 30m`, `150s` | Keep (5) |
| PERCENTAGE_PROGRESS | Progress percentages | `Progress: 75%` | Strip (1) |
| EXIT_CODE | Process exit codes | `0`, `1`, `139` | Keep (8) |
| KEYBOARD_INTERRUPT | Keyboard interrupt signals | `KeyboardInterrupt`, `SIGINT` | Keep (9) |
| MEMORY_USAGE | Memory usage indicators | `Mem: 4GB/8GB` | Keep (5) |
| RATIO_RATES | Ratio/rate values | `3:1`, `0.5:1`, `50%` | Keep (6) |

### NER Classification Workflow
```
1. Input terminal output text is processed by spaCy NER
2. Each token/entity is classified into one of 20+ entity types
3. Classification confidence scores are calculated (0.0-1.0)
4. High-confidence classifications (> 0.5 default) are assigned final types
5. Low-confidence classifications are routed to fallback classifier
6. Final entity types determine stripping/keeping decisions
```

### Custom Entity Addition
```yaml
custom_entities:
  - name: "DEBUG_PREFIX"
    pattern: "^DEBUG:"  # Regex pattern
    retention: "strip"  # or "keep"
  
  - name: "VERBOSE_MODE"
    pattern: "--verbose"
    retention: "keep"
  
  - name: "INTERACTIVE_PROMPT"
    pattern: ">>>"  # Python REPL prompt
    retention: "strip"
```

## ANSI Code Removal & Normalization (Phase 6 Component 2)

### ANSI Escape Code Patterns (40+ Patterns)
| Pattern Category | Example Patterns | Retention |
|-----------------|------------------|-----------|
| Basic Formatting | `\x1b[1m` (bold), `\x1b[0m` (reset) | Strip |
| Foreground Color | `\x1b[31m` (red), `\x1b[32m` (green) | Strip |
| Background Color | `\x1b[41m` (red bg), `\x1b[42m` (green bg) | Strip |
| Text Formatting | `\x1b[4m` (underline), `\x1b[7m` (reverse) | Strip |
| Cursor Movement | `\x1b[H` (home), `\x1b[2J` (clear screen) | Strip |
| Erase Operations | `\x1b[2K` (erase line), `\x1b[K` (erase line end) | Strip |
| Scroll Operations | `\x1b[S` (scroll up), `\x1b[T` (scroll down) | Strip |
| Cursor Position | `\x1b[row;colf` (position cursor) | Strip |
| Screen Clear | `\x1b[2J`, `\x1b[H` | Strip |
| Color Combinations | `\x1b[31;42m` (red on green) | Strip |
| Extended Colors | `\x1b[38;5;196m` (256-color) | Strip |

### ANSI Stripping Algorithm
```
Function: strip_ansi_codes()
Inputs:
  - terminal_text: raw terminal output text
  - options: {patterns_to_strip, preserve_essential, normalize}
Outputs:
  - stripped_text: terminal output without ANSI escape codes
  - removed_sequences: list of removed ANSI sequences
  - normalization_applied: whether any normalization was performed
  - preservation_report: report on what was preserved/stripped
```

### Normalization Features
- `normalize_whitespace": true (whether to normalize whitespace after stripping, default: true)
- `collapse_sequences": true (whether to collapse multiple stripping operations, default: true)
- `preserve_readable": true (whether to preserve human-readable portions, default: true)

## Repetitive Pattern Detection (Phase 6 Component 3)

### 100+ Repetitive Pattern Types
| Pattern Type | Example | Detection Method | Retention |
|--------------|---------|-----------------|-----------|
| Progress Spinners | `⠋⠙⠹⠸⠼⠴⠦⠧⠨⠩` | Entropy + regex | Strip (90%) |
| Status Dots | `.`, `.`, `.`, `.`, `.` | Count-based detection | Strip (85%) |
| Loading Dots | `. . . . . . . . . .` | Pattern recognition | Strip (90%) |
| Heartbeat Signals | `--`, `--`, `--` | Periodic pattern detection | Strip (80%) |
| Empty Lines | Blank lines between code | Line-count-based | Strip (70%) with preservation |
| Repeated Headers | `---`, `---`, `---` | Pattern repetition | Strip (85%) |
| Dividers | `=====`, `=====`, `=====` | Pattern recognition | Strip (80%) |
| API Response Repeats | JSON blocks repeated | Content-hash-based | Strip (95%) |
| Error Recurrence | Same error message repeated | Content-hash + frequency | Strip (85%) keep once |
| Spinner Cycles | Full spinner cycle detection | Cycle detection algorithm | Strip (90%) |
| Status Update Flood | Too many status updates | Frequency-based detection | Strip (70%) keep last |
| Log Repetition | Log messages repeated | Content-hash + frequency | Strip (90%) keep unique |
| Banner Repeats | ASCII art banners repeated | Image-hash-based | Strip (95%) |
| Prefix Repeats | Same prefix on every line | Pattern detection | Strip (85%) |

### Repetitive Pattern Detection Algorithm
```
Function: detect_repetitive_patterns()
Inputs:
  - text: terminal output text
  - options: {pattern_types, entropy_threshold, frequency_threshold}
Outputs:
  - detected_patterns: list of identified repetitive patterns
  - pattern_frequencies: how often each pattern repeats
  - stripping_decisions: per-pattern strip/keep decisions
  - preservation_report: what was preserved vs. stripped
  - reduction_percentage: calculated reduction (target: 20-30%)
```

### Frequency-Based Detection
```
1. Tokenize terminal output into segments
2. Calculate frequency of each unique segment
3. Identify segments exceeding frequency threshold (default: > 3 occurrences)
4. Group similar segments using fuzzy matching (80%+ similarity)
5. Mark for stripping while preserving first occurrence
6. Calculate reduction percentage based on stripped occurrences
```

## Noise Entropy Analysis (Phase 6 Component 4)

### Entropy Calculation
```
Function: calculate_noise_entropy()
Inputs:
  - text_segments: list of text segments from terminal output
  - options: {entropy_formula, threshold, unit}
Outputs:
  - entropy_scores: Shannon entropy per segment (0.0-5.0+ range)
  - high_entropy_segments: segments with entropy > threshold (default: > 3.0)
  - low_entropy_segments: segments with entropy <= threshold
  - entropy_distribution: histogram of entropy scores across all segments
  - reduction_suggestions: which high-entropy segments to strip
```

### Entropy Threshold Configuration
- `entropy_threshold`: 3.0 (Shannon entropy threshold, range: 2.0-4.5, default: 3.0)
- `entropy_formula`: "shannon" (shannon, normalized, custom)
- `unit": "bits" (entropy unit: "bits", "nats", "dits")
- `entropy_percentile": 75 (use 75th percentile as dynamic threshold, range: 50-99, default: 75)

### High-Entropy Segment Characteristics
- High entropy typically indicates: random-looking content, true noise, non-deterministic output
- Low entropy typically indicates: structured, predictable, potentially important content
- Entropy thresholds are context-sensitive (varies by terminal type and content)

### Entropy-Based Stripping Decisions
```
1. Calculate Shannon entropy per terminal output segment
2. Compare against threshold (default: 3.0 bits)
3. Segments above threshold: likely noise, candidate for stripping
4. Segments below threshold: potentially important, preserve
5. Apply edge case exceptions (some high-entropy = important, some low-entropy = noisy)
6. Calculate expected reduction percentage
```

## Agent-Specific Noise Profiles (Phase 6 Component 5)

### 50+ Agent Noise Profiles

#### Claude Code Noise Profile
```yaml
agent: "claude_code"
profile_name: "claude_code_terminal_noise"

noise_types:
  - entity_type: "ANSI_CODE"
    retention: "strip"
    priority: 1
  - entity_type: "STATUS_LINE"
    retention: "strip"
    priority: 1
  - entity_type: "TIMESTAMP"
    retention: "strip"
    priority: 1
  - entity_type: "PROGRESS_BAR"
    retention: "strip"
    priority: 2
  - entity_type: "ERROR_MESSAGE"
    retention: "keep"
    priority: 9
  - entity_type: "WARNING_MESSAGE"
    retention: "keep"
    priority: 8
  - entity_type: "COMMAND_OUTPUT"
    retention: "keep"
    priority: 9
  - entity_type: "EXIT_CODE"
    retention: "keep"
    priority: 8

noise_reduction_target: 70  # 70%+ reduction target
quality_preservation_target: 92  # 92%+ quality preservation
noise_profile_optimization: "adaptive"  # Adaptive based on feedback

claude_specific_patterns:
  - "^Clack:"  # Claude Code specific prefixes
  - "#.*claudedoc:"  # Claude Code comment patterns
  - "::::"  # Claude Code section dividers
```

